Installing the Maestro SDK

Integrate the MaestroKit Roku SDK into this Roku channel and get it rendering.

Everything you need is below — do not search the web or guess at API names.
Several steps contain non-obvious traps that are called out as CRITICAL. The
failures they describe are silent: the integration looks like it works.

## Prerequisites

You need the device IP and the developer password for sideloading. Ask me for
both if they are not already in the environment or a launch config — do not
assume the "rokudev" default.

## What the SDK is

A remote Roku ComponentLibrary. You load it by URL, wait for it, then create a
node from it. Nothing is installed or vendored into this repo.

  Library URL:  https://roku-sdk.us-central1-master.gcp.maestro.io/4.1.16/maestrokit.pkg
  Library name: MaestroPanelLib
  Node type:    MaestroPanelLib:MaestroPanel

Write plain BrightScript (.brs) + SceneGraph XML. This SDK is consumed as a
prebuilt library, so no BrighterScript toolchain is required.

Before you start: add a print to every observer callback you register, tagged
so you can grep it (e.g. "[maestro] loadStatus = "). I will ask you for the
ordered sequence of values at the end, and that is not recoverable after the
fact.

## Steps

1. In the component that owns your video view, create the ComponentLibrary and
   observe its loadStatus:

     m.componentLibrary = createObject("roSGNode", "ComponentLibrary")
     m.componentLibrary.observeField("loadStatus", "onLibraryLoadStatusChanged")
     m.componentLibrary.uri = "https://roku-sdk.us-central1-master.gcp.maestro.io/4.1.16/maestrokit.pkg"

   CRITICAL: register the observer BEFORE setting uri. Assigning uri starts the
   download, so the reverse order registers too late to ever see the "loading"
   transition — you will only ever observe "ready". Some published Maestro
   samples have these two lines the other way round; they are wrong.

   loadStatus is one of "loading", "ready", "failed". The library node does not
   need to be appended to the scene.

   A "failed" on the first launch after a sideload is usually transient
   (network congestion, not a code path). Retry once before concluding the
   load path is broken.

2. When loadStatus is "ready", create the panel, register observers, then
   append it to the scene:

     m.lib = createObject("roSGNode", "MaestroPanelLib:MaestroPanel")
     m.lib.observeField("showPanel", "onShowPanel")
     m.lib.observeField("handleLoadingSceneAction", "onHandleLoadingSceneAction")
     m.lib.observeField("focusedTabItem", "onFocusedTabItem")
     m.lib.observeField("trackingEvent", "onTrackingEvent")
     m.lib.observeField("userRequestedLogin", "onUserRequestedLogin")
     m.lib.observeField("errorInformation", "onErrorInformation")
     m.top.appendChild(m.lib)

   CRITICAL: append the panel to the scene BEFORE setting config. Setting
   config kicks off network requests that require the node to already be in the
   scene graph. Getting this backwards is the most common integration failure.

   Register every observer here, before step 3. The SDK validates config
   synchronously during the `m.lib.config = {...}` assignment, so
   errorInformation fires before the next line of your own code runs. Anything
   you want to correlate those callbacks against — a timer, a log prefix, a
   start timestamp — must exist before the config line, not after it.

3. Size and configure it. Use these demo credentials verbatim — they point at a
   live Maestro demo page and need no account:

     m.lib.width = 1280
     m.lib.height = 720
     m.lib.config = {
       siteID: "69b2f133e8117ec6536d59a1"
       pageID: "6aa966d669342216ada5a4b8"
       useProdEnv: false
     }

   All three values are load-bearing. useProdEnv: false is not a debug nicety —
   it is half the condition that renders the Hello World screen. Do not change
   it to true while you are still verifying.

   To point this at real content afterwards, replace siteID and pageID with the
   ids from your own Maestro site — both appear in the browser URL when you open
   it. With your own site you get your configured panels instead of the Hello
   World screen, which is the normal end state; Hello World is the smoke test,
   not the destination. Everything else in this integration stays the same.

   Config accepts more than these three keys — authToken and swid for an
   authenticated user, eventID instead of pageID for a live or VOD event. Each
   panel reads what it needs from this object, so pass what your panels require.

4. Wire show. The panel is hidden until you ask for it:

     m.lib.showPanel = true
     m.lib.callFunc("setPanelFocus")

   Bind this to a key or button that already exists in this app — pick the most
   natural trigger in the current code rather than adding new chrome.

5. Wire hide and focus return. This step has two traps; both are silent.

   The SDK's documented signal for return-focus is handleLoadingSceneAction.
   Implement it, because real panels do emit it:

     sub onHandleLoadingSceneAction(event)
       if event.getData() = "returnFocus"
         focusChannelUI()
       end if
     end sub

   TRAP 1 — with the demo credentials above it never fires. You land on the
   HelloWorld fallback screen, and in this SDK build "returnFocus" is only
   emitted when an overlay releases focus, never when a user backs out of a
   panel. Back will bubble past the panel to your scene and exit the channel.
   So handle Back yourself as well:

     if key = "back" and m.lib <> invalid and m.lib.showPanel
       m.lib.showPanel = false
       return true
     end if

   TRAP 2 — CRITICAL: the showPanel observer fires inline, while the SDK still
   holds focus, so setFocus called from inside it silently does nothing.
   Two things are required:

     - Defer the focus restore by a frame (a 0.05s one-shot Timer).
     - Focus a concrete focusable node in your own UI. setFocus on the Scene
       itself will NOT reclaim focus out of the panel's subtree.

   Get either wrong and the drawer still closes correctly — it just swallows
   the next OK key-down and never reopens. It looks like it works. Verify by
   printing hasFocus on your node and isInFocusChain on the panel after the
   restore; you want true and false respectively.

6. Shrink the video out from behind the panel. The drawer opens on the right,
   so a full-screen video sits underneath it. Add two helpers and call them from
   the showPanel observer:

     sub minVideo()
       m.video.translation = [50, 200]
       m.video.width = 1184
       m.video.height = 666
     end sub

     sub maxVideo()
       m.video.translation = [0, 0]
       m.video.width = 1920
       m.video.height = 1080
     end sub

   Call minVideo() in the branch of onShowPanel where the panel became visible
   and maxVideo() in the other branch, next to the focus handling from step 5.
   Also call maxVideo() during teardown.

   Those numbers are the values used for the ESPN and Fox panels. Panel width is
   client-specific — Paramount's panel is wider and needs [0, 137] / 1436x807 —
   so treat them as a starting point, not a constant. There is no SDK field that
   reports the drawer's width; the host is expected to know it.

   A Video node honours width/height/translation on its own; you do not need to
   change any node attribute to make this work. Measured on device: geometry is
   applied exactly, to the pixel, with enableUI at its default of true, on a
   node that never takes focus.

   If a node is actively rendering Roku's built-in player UI it may behave
   differently — enableUI only draws that UI while the node has focus, so this
   would only apply to a player the viewer can focus. That case has not been
   measured. Do not pre-emptively change enableUI to work around it. If the
   geometry genuinely does not apply on this app, say so and report whether the
   video node ever receives focus, rather than changing attributes to chase it.

   Separately, and not as a fix: the SDK's own test harness declares its Video
   node enableUI="false" focusable="false" enableTrickPlay="false", because it
   draws its own controls. That is the normal shape for a production client,
   but it is a product decision about playback controls, not a requirement for
   resizing. Leave this app's attributes alone unless asked.

   Snapping the geometry is fine and is what the SDK's own docs show. If you
   want it to look deliberate, drive translation/width/height through an
   Animation with a Vector2DFieldInterpolator instead — that is what the
   harness does, over 0.3s with an outQuad ease.

7. Push the player timecode. The SDK gates overlays on it, so without this the
   Hello World overlay never appears:

     ' On the video's position tick:
     sub onVideoPosition(event)
       t = createObject("roDateTime")
       ms& = (t.AsSecondsLong() * 1000) + t.GetMilliseconds()
       m.lib.callFunc("onPlayerTimeCodeUpdated", ms&)
     end sub

   Observe the Video node's "position" field to drive this. The value must be
   an epoch-milliseconds wall clock as a LongInteger (&), not seconds-into-
   stream. For this demo, current wall clock is correct. (A real client
   integration would push the wall-clock time the current frame corresponds to:
   broadcast-start-epoch + position * 1000.)

8. Tear down when playback ends and when the user leaves the video view — this
   flushes analytics and destroys nodes:

     m.lib.callFunc("destroySDK")
     m.top.removeChild(m.lib)
     m.lib = invalid
     m.componentLibrary = invalid

   If this channel has no exit path, observe the Video node's "state" field and
   tear down on "finished" and "error".

   Stub the remaining observer callbacks (onFocusedTabItem, onTrackingEvent,
   onUserRequestedLogin, onErrorInformation) as print-only subs. They must exist or observeField
   fails at runtime, but nothing is required of them here.

   Print the payload in onErrorInformation rather than ignoring it — that is
   where the SDK reports a rejected config, and it is the first thing to check
   if nothing renders.

   Note that onFocusedTabItem is the one of the other three that actually fires —
   on every panel show, within ~15ms of showPanel = true, because the SDK
   focuses its nav tab. onTrackingEvent and onUserRequestedLogin stay silent in
   this scenario. If you are using callback silence to judge whether the SDK is
   alive, onFocusedTabItem is the one that talks.

## Beyond this integration

Not needed for Hello World, but this is the surface you will reach for next, so
do not invent your own mechanisms for them: setDataToPanel (push data into a
panel), openToPanel (open a specific tab), userSettings (per-panel toggles such
as hide_panel and disable_overlays), displayQRCodes, and onPanelEvent /
userViewedPanel (panel interaction callbacks).

## Constraints

- Use ONLY the fields and functions named above. Do not add `failedClipId`,
  `showLoading`, or `authenticateUser` — they appear in some older Maestro docs
  and do not exist in the SDK. Referencing them will not error; they will just
  silently never fire.
- Do not restructure this app's existing focus or key handling, or its video
  layout, beyond what steps 4-8 require.

## Expected log noise — do NOT debug these

On a fully successful run you will see, and should ignore:

    BRIGHTSCRIPT: ERROR: roSGNode: Failed to create roSGNode with type chat
    Warnings occurred while creating XML component HelloWorld

The first is the point of the exercise: the demo page is configured with a
panel this generic build does not contain, which is what triggers the Hello
World screen. It surfaces as a BRIGHTSCRIPT: ERROR but is expected.

## Then verify

Sideload and open the view that owns the panel.

Expected:
  - A Maestro drawer opens over your content and takes focus, showing
    "HELLO, WORLD! / Panel component has been installed correctly", listing
    "Chat" as an unsupported panel.
  - 10-15 seconds after the panel builds, a Hello World overlay appears
    ("Hello, World!" / "Successful SDK integration"), stays up for about 10
    seconds, then dismisses itself. This requires step 7.
  - The overlay is independent of the drawer: it appears whether the drawer is
    open or closed. Do not sit with the drawer open waiting for it — that is
    the wrong layer.
  - Back closes the drawer and returns focus to your UI, and OK reopens it.
    Test open -> back -> reopen -> back, not just one open.
  - The video shrinks to the left of the drawer when it opens and returns to
    full screen when it closes. Confirm this by looking at the screen. A log
    line only proves the fields were assigned, so it cannot confirm the video
    moved. A dev screenshot can: it does not capture video *content*, but it
    does capture the video *window* as a black rectangle against the SDK's
    background, measurable to the pixel. Report the measured rect in both
    states.

How to capture evidence:
  - Screenshot: POST to /plugin_inspect with mysubmit=Screenshot (digest auth,
    user rokudev), then GET /pkgs/dev.jpg.
  - The dev screenshot does NOT capture the video plane. A black background is
    expected and is not a failure.
  - Port 8085 accepts a single connection and sideloading drops it. If an IDE
    debugger holds it you will lose the log lines you were asked to report —
    attach a reconnecting logger before you deploy.

Then report:
  - the loadStatus values you observed, in order
  - whether the panel appeared, took focus, and returned focus on back, and
    whether it reopened afterwards
  - whether the overlay appeared, and how long after the panel built
  - what onErrorInformation reported. With the demo config you WILL see
    "No authToken set in config" and "No SWID set in config" on every launch —
    the demo page is unauthenticated and these are informational, not failures.
    "No siteId set in config" is the real failure signal; that one should not
    appear.

Do not report success unless you have actually seen the panel and the overlay
render on a device.
